  This paper presents a new scheme called Fault Location Identification (FLI) for DDSs that uses real-time data from smart meters and phasor measurement units (PMUs). The proposed method relies on synchronized voltage magnitudes to detect single-phase faults and can be applied to unbalanced fault scenarios, including phase-to-phase and three-phase faults. It achieves this by calculating the difference in voltage magnitudes between two substations and comparing them to predetermined thresholds, determined through simulation of various fault conditions, including location and type. The effectiveness of the FLI scheme is validated through practical applications in a 12-kV DDS.  The proposed method will enable faster fault identification and repair, contributing to enhanced reliability and efficiency of DDSs. This will also facilitate the development of prescription systems that can accurately locate faults, thus streamlining the process for distribution automations and with reduced dependency on field testing. Furthermore, this method will empower the creation of self-healing capabilities that can detect and resolve faults without human intervention, leading to improved performance.  The proposed method has been successfully tested and validated through practical applications in a 12-kV DDS.  The results confirm that FLI can accurately determine fault locations down to a specific section or bus, with a maximum error of 2%.  This method has been found to be effective even under distorted and unbalanced conditions, and its reliability was further verified through the application of various fault scenarios, including single-phase, phase-to-phase, and three-phase faults.  This method has been found to be reliable and efficient for fault location identification in DDSs and can be replicated in other systems of any voltage class.  The simplicity and cost-effectiveness of the proposed method will facilitate widespread adoption and integration with other system operations.  Th